 "answer": "Yes, the haze in KL is bad right now. It's in the \"unhealthy\" range, though not the worst in the country. The latest reports I found are from 17 October 2026, so check a live source for current numbers.\n\n**Latest readings (DOE, as of 9.30am on 17 Oct):**\n- Cheras recorded an API of 162 and Batu Muda 159. Putrajaya was also at 162.\n- Nationwide, 24 areas were in the unhealthy range. Pasir Gudang, Johor was highest at 196.\n- This is an improvement on 16 Oct, when about 30 areas were unhealthy and Pasir Gudang reached 207, which is \"very unhealthy\".\n- Nearby Selangor stations were also unhealthy on 16 Oct: Johan Setia (184), Klang (176) and Petaling Jaya (173). Schools in eight Selangor districts were closed that Friday.\n\n**What the numbers mean:**\n- 0–50 is good.\n- 51–100 is moderate.\n- 101–200 is unhealthy.\n- 201–300 is very unhealthy.\n- Above 300 is hazardous.\n\nThe haze is reported to come from fires in Indonesia.\n\n**What to do:**\n- Wear a well-fitted N95 or KN95 mask outdoors. Surgical masks don't filter fine particles well.\n- Limit strenuous outdoor activity. Children, the elderly, and people with asthma, heart or lung conditions should be especially careful.\n- Keep windows closed. Use an air purifier or air-conditioning on recirculate if you have them.\n\nFor live station readings, check the DOE's APIMS site (apims.doe.gov.my), hazemonitor.com, or hazenow.app/malaysia/kuala-lumpur. Readings change through the day and differ by station.",
